  "text": "Solution 2 most likely has the correct answer, 12, though I could not verify it fully without tools.\n\n**Solution 1 (6):** Its map checks out. $g(0)=0$, $g(1)=1$, $g(2)=2$, the multipliers are all $-2$, and the preimages $4/3$, $2/3$, $\\infty$ are correct. The 2-cycle discriminant is $-12$, and the second-level preimages are irrational as stated. But the argument only shows that this particular map has six rational preperiodic points. It does not show that six is the maximum over all $S_3$-symmetric maps over $\\mathbb{Q}$, and the \"structural obstruction\" claim is asserted rather than proved.\n\n**Solution 2 (12):** The structure is plausible. $S_3$ acts on the set of rational preperiodic points, and orbits have size 3 or 6, so the counting is consistent. Its explicit construction is missing, and the claim that all six period-3 points can be rational is unverified. Still, this matches my recollection of Doyle's work on quadratic rational maps with nonabelian automorphism group, which I believe gives 12 as the sharp bound.\n\n**Solution 3 (4):** This is wrong. $f(z)=1/z^2$ has an abelian automorphism group, so it is not an example of the $S_3$ case. The claim that every $S_3$ map is conjugate to $1/z^2$ is false, and the bound it relies on is not justified.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
